<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-power-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Power Series</title>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x)=e^x</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L(x)</m> should satisfy <m>L(0)=f(0)</m> and <m>L^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></p>
      </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a quadratic approximation <m>L_2(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L_2(x)</m> should satisfy 
          <ul>
            <li><m>L_2(0)=f(0)</m> </li>
            <li><m>L_2^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_2^{\prime \prime}(0)=f^{\prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a degree 3 approximation <m>L_3(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p><m>L_3(x)</m> should satisfy 
          <ul>
            <li><m>L_3(0)=f(0)</m> </li>
            <li><m>L_3^\prime(0)=f^\prime(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_3^{\prime \prime}(0)=f^{\prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
            <li><m>L_3^{\prime \prime \prime }(0)=f^{\prime \prime \prime}(0)</m></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Evaluate your three approximations at <m>x=0.1</m>.
        How do they compare to <m>e^{0.1}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a degree <m>n</m> approximation <m>L_n(x)</m> to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Plot your degree <m>n</m> approximation in Desmos alongside the function <m>f(x)=e^x</m>.
         Add a slider and observe what happens as <m>n</m> increases? 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <definition xml:id="def-Taylor-polynomial">
    <statement>
      <p>
          Let <m>f(x)</m> be <m>n</m>-times differentiable at <m>x=c</m>.
          The <term>Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=c</m></term>
          is the polynomial <m>\displaystyle T_n(x)=\sum _{k=1} ^n \dfrac{f^{(k)}(c)(x-c)^k}{k!|}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <observation>
    <p>By construction, if <m>T_n(x)</m> is the degree <m>n</m>
      Taylor polynomial for <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=c</m>,
      then <m>T_n ^{(i)}(c)=f^{(i)}(c)</m> for all <m>0 \leq i \leq n</m>.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <definition xml:id="def-power-series">
    <statement>
      <p>
         A power series is a function of the form 
         <md>P(x)=\sum _{n=0} ^\infty a_n (x-c)^n</md>
         for some real numbers <m>c</m> and <m>a_n</m>.
         That is, <m>P(x)</m> is defined to be the value that 
         the series converges to where it converges, and is undefined
         elsewhere.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <activity>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the power series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \frac{x^n}{n!}</m>
        converges everywhere, i.e. for every value of <m>x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </activity>

</section>